  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 712 AM EDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nSurface high pressure will continue over the Ohio Valley today \nleaving mostly clear skies. Temperatures are expected to warm into \nthe upper 70s and low 80s across the region today. As the surface \nhigh begins to move eastward and winds start to shift southerly, \nclouds will move in from the NE. Nighttime lows are expected to be \nin the mid 50s as clouds will prevent some radiational cooling. By \nWednesday afternoon, steady SSW winds return to the region bringing \nwarmer temperatures and moisture. Maximum temperatures for Wednesday \nafternoon are expected to increase into the upper 80s across the \narea.\n\nAs the surface high continues east out of the area, upper level \nridging amplifies as a low pressure system is over the Central \nPlains. Return flow will keep temperatures on Thursday in the upper \n80s with a 70% chance of hitting 90 degrees. Additionally moisture \nadvection continues into the Ohio Valley with dewpoints nearing 70. \nThe afore mentioned low will begin to lift into the jet stream \nleaving a shortwave disturbance to move into the region. An \nassociated cold front will push south, becoming a lifting mechanism \nfor developing rain showers. As the front moves through on Friday, \nrain showers are expected to be widespread through most of the day. \nSevere threat still remains low as instability lacks due to the lack \nof heating. After the cold front pushes through the area, another \nshortwave disturbance will keep showers lingering Saturday. With \nPWATS increasing above 2 inches, rainfall is expected to be heavy \nwith QPF ranging 2-4 inches over several days. \n\nOn the other side of things, post frontal boundary temperatures will \ntake a slight dip back into the 70s. Areas especially in south \ncentral KY have the best chance of seeing upper 80s on Friday, \nhowever the northern part of the CWA may only see max temperatures \nin the upper 70s and low 80s. As cloud cover and cooler air sets in, \ntemperatures on Saturday will likely stay in the mid to low 70s. By \nSunday an area of high pressure will build back into the region \nwhich will begin to clear out some cloud cover for the later half of \nthe weekend into the beginning of the week. The return of northerly \nflow will keep daytime temperature pleasant in the 70s.",
